rcq("R01-D-08-4", "Inference", "Difficult",
    "The author would most likely agree that a relational egalitarian, considering a society that has abolished titles but retains very large differences in wealth, should",
    "ask whether those differences have become large enough to give some people standing over others.",
    [("regard that society as fully equal in the relevant sense, since no one in it holds any formal rank.",
      "Paragraph 5 says the relational egalitarian 'owes us an account of when such differences begin to corrode equal standing', so the question is not closed by abolishing titles."),
     ("favour reducing everyone's wealth to the level of the poorest, since this would make all the citizens equal.",
      "This is levelling down, which the relational view does not need; paragraph 5 says paving gardens 'neither creates nor abolishes any relation of rank'."),
     ("set aside questions of income entirely, since the relational view is concerned with standing and not distribution.",
      "Paragraph 4 says inequalities of resources matter on this view 'because, beyond some point, they turn into inequalities of standing', so income is not irrelevant.")],
    "Paragraph 4 says resource inequalities matter 'chiefly because, beyond some point, they turn into inequalities of standing', and paragraph 5 uses exactly this case, a society without titles but with 'vast differences in income', to say the relational egalitarian must explain when such differences corrode equal standing.",
    "Choosing the option that sets income aside, from the slogan that the relational view is 'not in how goods are distributed'.",
    "One distractor rests on a half-true reading of paragraph 4's opening; rejecting it needs the qualification at the paragraph's end and the open question in paragraph 5.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

rcq("R01-D-08-5", "Main idea / primary purpose", "Difficult",
    "Which of the following best states the main argument of the passage?",
    "The levelling-down objection can be answered, but the answer makes equality impersonal, which favours a relational account despite its open questions.",
    [("The levelling-down objection shows that egalitarians should give priority to benefits for the worse off rather than value equality as such.",
      "This is the response of the many philosophers described in paragraph 2; the author goes on to argue that the objection 'is less decisive than it is usually taken to be'."),
     ("The levelling-down objection is decisive against every form of egalitarianism except one concerned with relations of rank between persons.",
      "Paragraph 3 argues that even distributive egalitarianism survives the objection ('This defence succeeds'), so the objection is not decisive against it."),
     ("Equality cannot be a genuine value in its own right, because every genuine value must make situations better for some person.",
      "The author says only that 'Many will find such a value hard to care about'; the passage does not assert that impersonal values are impossible.")],
    "Paragraph 3 answers the objection, paragraph 4 says the answer 'succeeds, but at a price' by making equality impersonal and introduces the relational view, and paragraph 5 says that view escapes the objection while owing an account of distribution. The key traces this whole movement.",
    "Choosing the prioritarian retreat because it is presented first and at length, or overstating the cost of the defence into a denial that equality is a value.",
    "Each distractor captures one stage of a five-paragraph argument; only the key holds the defence, its cost and the hedged preference together.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)
